<article dtd-version="1.3" article-type="meeting-abstract" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="hwp">ascomtg</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="pmc">ascomtg</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">ASCOMTG</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Journal of Clinical Oncology</journal-title><abbrev-journal-title>ASCO MEETING ABSTRACTS</abbrev-journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0732-183X</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1527-7755</issn><publisher><publisher-name>American Society of Clinical Oncology</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="meeting-id">335</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="session-id">17304</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="session-participation-id">8019600</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="temporary-abstract-id">30296</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="custom" custom-type="abstract-id">rw30296</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/s41417-018-0065-7</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Biology - Cancer</subject></subj-group><series-title>2026 ASCO Annual Meeting (May 29 - June 2, 2026)</series-title><series-text>Publication Only</series-text></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Upregulating microRNA-498 inhibits gastric cancer proliferation invasion and chemoresistance through inverse interaction of Bmi1</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib id="C8019600" contrib-type="presenter"><name><surname>Zhao</surname><given-names>Tiancheng</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8019600" /></contrib><contrib id="C8019601" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Chen</surname><given-names>Yahong</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8019600" /></contrib><contrib id="C8019602" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Sheng</surname><given-names>Shihou</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8019600" /></contrib><contrib id="C8019603" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Wu</surname><given-names>YUanyu</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8019600" /></contrib><contrib id="C8019604" contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Zhang</surname><given-names>Tao</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="A8019600" /></contrib><aff id="A8019600">Department of Endoscopy Center, China-Japan Union Hospital of Jilin University, Changchun, 130000, China; Department of Gastrointestinal surgery, China-Japan Union Hospital of Jilin University, Changchun, 130000, China;</aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><year>2018</year></pub-date><fpage>rw30296</fpage><lpage>rw30296</lpage><permissions><copyright-statement>ASCO-style conversion for internal testing; source rights remain with the original publisher.</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2018</copyright-year></permissions><abstract><p><bold>rw30296</bold></p><p>This study aimed to analyze the functions of microRNA 498 (miR-498) on gastric cancer (GC) cell proliferation migration and cisplatin chemosensitivity. QTR-PCR found that miR-498 was markedly downregulated in GC cell lines and human GC tumors. It was discover that, lentivirus-mediated miR-498 overexpression inhibited cancer cell proliferations in vitro and in vivo, invasion and cisplatin chemoresistance. Bmi1 was demonstrated to be directly regulated by miR-498 in GC cell lines. Moreover, Bmi1 upregulation was found to reverse the tumor-suppressing functions of miR-498 in GC. Therefore, this study presented evidence showing miR-498 expression decreased in GC, and overexpressing miR-498 had significant inhibitory effects on GC development, likely through the inverse interaction of Bmi1.</p></abstract><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>source-row-number</meta-name><meta-value>196</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-record-id</meta-name><meta-value>30296.0</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-journal</meta-name><meta-value>Cancer Gene Therapy</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-publisher</meta-name><meta-value>Springer - Nature Publishing Group</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-country</meta-name><meta-value>China</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-article-type</meta-name><meta-value>Research Article;</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>manual-verdict</meta-name><meta-value>YES</meta-value></custom-meta><custom-meta><meta-name>source-reason</meta-name><meta-value>Duplication of Data;Duplication of/in Image;Investigation by Journal/Publisher;Investigation by Third Party;Paper Mill;Unreliable Results and/or Conclusions;</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front></article>
